# 09 — Screen, touch and buttons with Scenic

[Scenic](https://hexdocs.pm/scenic) is an Elixir UI framework. A *scene*
describes what's on screen as a *graph* of primitives (text, rectangles,
circles…) and receives input: touches and button presses.

## Free the screen

The IEx console is drawn on the screen too. Detach it while Scenic runs:

```elixir
fbcon =
  Path.wildcard("/sys/class/vtconsole/vtcon*")
  |> Enum.find(&(File.read!(Path.join(&1, "name")) =~ "frame buffer"))

File.write!(Path.join(fbcon, "bind"), "0")
File.write!("/sys/class/graphics/fb0/blank", "0")
```

## A scene

This scene shows a title and a status line. Touching the screen draws a
circle and shows the coordinates; the volume and power buttons show
their name.

```elixir
defmodule Demo.Scene do
  use Scenic.Scene
  alias Scenic.Graph
  import Scenic.Primitives

  @impl Scenic.Scene
  def init(scene, _param, _opts) do
    {width, height} = scene.viewport.size

    graph =
      Graph.build(font: :roboto, font_size: 72)
      |> rect({width, height}, fill: {20, 24, 60})
      |> text("Hello from Nerves!", fill: :white, translate: {60, 200})
      |> text("Touch the screen or press a button",
        id: :status,
        font_size: 44,
        fill: :light_gray,
        translate: {60, 300}
      )

    request_input(scene, [:cursor_button, :key])
    {:ok, scene |> assign(graph: graph) |> push_graph(graph)}
  end

  @impl Scenic.Scene
  def handle_input({:cursor_button, {_button, 1, _mods, {x, y}}}, _id, scene) do
    scene.assigns.graph
    |> circle(40, fill: :yellow, translate: {x, y})
    |> Graph.modify(:status, &text(&1, "Touch at #{round(x)}, #{round(y)}"))
    |> show(scene)
  end

  def handle_input({:key, {key, 1, _mods}}, _id, scene) do
    scene.assigns.graph
    |> Graph.modify(:status, &text(&1, "Button: #{key}"))
    |> show(scene)
  end

  def handle_input(_input, _id, scene), do: {:noreply, scene}

  defp show(graph, scene), do: {:noreply, scene |> assign(graph: graph) |> push_graph(graph)}
end
```

## Show it

A *viewport* connects a scene to a display. `Scenic.Driver.Local` renders
to the phone's screen and reads the touchscreen and buttons.

```elixir
{:ok, viewport} =
  Scenic.ViewPort.start(
    name: :fp3_screen,
    size: {1080, 2160},
    default_scene: Demo.Scene,
    drivers: [[module: Scenic.Driver.Local, name: :fp3_screen_driver]]
  )
```

Now touch the screen and press the buttons. Edit the scene and run both
cells again to try your own graph (stop the viewport first, below).

## Stop

```elixir
Scenic.ViewPort.stop(viewport)
File.write!(Path.join(fbcon, "bind"), "1")
```
